  "navLabel": "8. Quality",
  "contentHtml": "<h1 class=\"module-heading\">Chapter 8: Quality</h1><div class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"card-title\">Purpose and SnakeBite example</h2><p>The quality practice defines the characteristics products must have and how they will be checked. Maya says SnakeBite should run smoothly on a phone. Zosia turns that expectation into measurable acceptance criteria and quality criteria in product descriptions. Kamil runs frame rate and unit tests as quality control. Independent quality assurance examines whether the agreed methods are suitable and used consistently. A passing automated test alone does not constitute formal product acceptance.</p><p>The project product description captures the overall product and its acceptance criteria; an individual product description sets criteria and quality methods for a component. The quality management approach defines the project’s quality arrangements. The quality register records planned checks and results. The product register tracks product status and approvals. A review by someone independent of the producer strengthens confidence when required.</p></div><figure class=\"study-figure\"><div class=\"study-flow\"><div class=\"study-step\"><span>1</span><strong>User expectations → acceptance criteria</strong></div><div class=\"study-step\"><span>2</span><strong>Product descriptions → quality criteria</strong></div><div class=\"study-step\"><span>3</span><strong>Checks → quality register</strong></div><div class=\"study-step\"><span>4</span><strong>Approval → product register</strong></div></div><figcaption><strong>How to read this sequence:</strong> Follow the steps left to right. Each step supplies information for the next decision at the appropriate management level.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"card-title\">Key terms, responsibilities and management products</h2><p><strong>Quality planning:</strong> agree user quality expectations, acceptance criteria, standards, methods, responsibilities and tolerances. <strong>Quality control:</strong> inspect or test products and record results. <strong>Quality assurance:</strong> independently confirm that quality arrangements provide confidence. Project assurance represents the board’s interests and is a different governance role. Service level agreements may contribute quality requirements, but they are not the whole quality plan.</p><div class=\"definition-item\"><strong>What are user quality expectations?</strong><p>A high level description of what users expect from the finished project product.</p></div><div class=\"definition-item\"><strong>What is a quality tolerance?</strong><p>Permitted variation around a quality criterion, where one has been agreed.</p></div><div class=\"definition-item\"><strong>Who agrees acceptance criteria?</strong><p>The user and business interests approve criteria through the project’s agreed authority.</p></div><div class=\"definition-item\"><strong>Quality assurance versus project assurance?</strong><p>Quality assurance reviews quality arrangements independently; project assurance checks the project for the board.</p></div><div class=\"definition-item\"><strong>Quality register versus product register?</strong><p>The first records checks and their results; the second tracks product status and approval.</p></div></div><div class=\"card quiz-section\"><h2 class=\"card-title\">SnakeBite scenario: review the decisions</h2><div id=\"m7-story-quiz\"></div></div><div class=\"hard-knowledge-header\"><div class=\"hard-knowledge-title\">Foundation exam practice</div></div><div class=\"quiz-section\"><div id=\"m7-exam-quiz\"></div></div><div class=\"flashcards-container\" id=\"m7-flashcards\"></div>",
